  "content": {
    "id": "p13-q14-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "14",
    "prompt": "Suppose $n\\in\\mathbb N$ and $n\\ge3$. Prove that the numbers\n$$n!+2,\\ n!+3,\\ n!+4,\\ n!+5,\\ \\ldots,\\ n!+n$$\nare all composite. This shows that there exist arbitrarily long runs of consecutive composite numbers. That is, there are arbitrarily large gaps between prime numbers.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "For $2\\le k\\le n$, $k$ is a proper divisor of $n!+k$. Thus the list contains $n-1$ consecutive composite numbers, and its length is unbounded.",
      "worked": "Let $k$ be any integer with $2\\le k\\le n$. Since $k$ is a factor of $n!$, write $n!=kq$ for a positive integer $q$. Then\n$$\\begin{align*}n!+k&=kq+k\\\\n!+k&=k(q+1).\\end{align*}$$\nBoth factors exceed $1$, so $n!+k$ is composite. This applies to every listed number.\n\nThere are $n-1$ consecutive integers from $n!+2$ to $n!+n$. Since $n$ can be arbitrarily large, these runs of composite numbers can be arbitrarily long, establishing arbitrarily large gaps between primes.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 68
  },
